// Copyright (c) 2026 Roshan. All rights reserved.// Proprietary license — see LICENSE file for details.package com.roshan.persona.automation.modelimport com.roshan.persona.brain.skill.SystemOperation/** * NOUS — Automation actions (Module 8). * * Sealed hierarchy describing WHAT to do when an [AutomationRule]'s trigger * fires. Each variant maps to a concrete executor in * [com.roshan.persona.automation.engine.AutomationEngine]: * *   - [SystemAction]  → delegates to [com.roshan.persona.brain.skill.executor.SystemOperationExecutor] *                       (the real Android implementation in :feature:system). *   - [SkillAction]   → looks up a [com.roshan.persona.brain.skill.BrainSkill] by ID *                       via [com.roshan.persona.brain.skill.registry.SkillRegistry] *                       and invokes it. (Skill invocation happens via a *                       BrainEvent emission so the Brain orchestrator picks it *                       up on its next routing cycle — direct invocation *                       requires constructing a full BrainContext, which is *                       out-of-scope for Module 8.) *   - [SpeakAction]   → uses Android `TextToSpeech` to speak [text] aloud. *   - [NotifyAction]  → posts a high-priority notification via `NotificationManager`. *   - [ChainAction]   → executes [actions] in sequence; one failure does NOT *                       abort the chain (each result is recorded + surfaced *                       in the worker's output). * * Strategy reference: §2.2 — Automation is one of four Brain input modalities. * This module turns user-defined "WHEN X THEN Y" rules into scheduled Android * work that fires actions on the user's behalf. * * @see <a href="docs/strategy/NOUS-Strategy-2-14-extracted.txt">§2.2</a> */sealed interface AutomationAction {    /**     * Execute a [SystemOperation] via the System stack (Module 10).     *     * Example: `SystemAction(SystemOperation.SetTorch(on = true))` — turns     * the torch on when the trigger fires.     *     * The engine calls     * `SystemOperationExecutor.execute(operation)` — which routes through     * :feature:system's handler registry (TorchHandler, VolumeHandler, etc.).     */    data class SystemAction(        val operation: SystemOperation,    ) : AutomationAction    /**     * Invoke a Brain skill by ID with the given [params].     *     * Example: `SkillAction("call", mapOf("contact" to "mom"))` — calls mom     * when the trigger fires.     *     * The engine emits a BrainEvent so the Brain orchestrator routes the     * synthetic intent on its next cycle. Direct synchronous skill execution     * requires constructing a full BrainContext (with persona, ambient     * snapshot, budget, etc.), which is left for a future revision.     *     * @param skillId the registered Brain skill ID (e.g., "call", "send_sms").     * @param params  skill parameters (slot values — e.g., "contact" → "mom").     */    data class SkillAction(        val skillId: String,        val params: Map<String, String>,    ) : AutomationAction    /**     * Speak [text] aloud via Android `TextToSpeech`.     *     * Uses the device's default TTS engine. The engine initializes TTS on     * first use, calls `speak(...)` with `QUEUE_ADD`, and returns immediately     * — speech playback continues in the background.     *     * @param text the text to speak (already localized by the caller).     */    data class SpeakAction(        val text: String,    ) : AutomationAction    /**     * Post a high-priority notification to the system tray.     *     * Uses `NotificationCompat` + the `nous_automation` notification channel     * (created lazily on first use). Notification ID is derived from the     * rule ID's hashCode for stable identity across re-fires.     *     * @param title   notification title (e.g., "Morning Briefing").     * @param message notification body text.     */    data class NotifyAction(        val title: String,        val message: String,    ) : AutomationAction    /**     * Execute multiple actions in sequence.     *     * One action's failure does NOT abort the chain — every action is     * attempted, and failures are recorded in the worker's output log.     *     * Example: `ChainAction([NotifyAction(...), SpeakAction(...)])` — posts     * a notification AND speaks the message aloud.     *     * @param actions ordered list of actions to execute.     */    data class ChainAction(        val actions: List<AutomationAction>,    ) : AutomationAction {        init {            require(actions.isNotEmpty()) { "ChainAction must have at least one action" }        }    }}